# =============================================================================
# apps/mobile/.env.example — the schema for apps/mobile/.env
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored (root .gitignore `.env`) and
# is NOT shipped into the EAS build archive (see `.easignore`), so it is
# per-machine state only.
#
# PLACEHOLDER VALUES ONLY IN THIS FILE. It is committed and the repo is public.
#
# ── What is NOT here, and why ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The production API host, the Naver Maps client ID and the Google OAuth web
# client ID used to live in `.env`. They are now committed constants in
# `apps/mobile/config/constants.js`, which explains per value why each is safe
# in a public repo.
#
# The reason is not convenience. Both eas-cli and eoas evaluate this project's
# app config with `EXPO_NO_DOTENV=1`, so on a release path Expo reads no dotenv
# file at all — a value survived there only because somebody remembered a
# `source .env` line in a shell script. Forgetting exactly that line is what
# published OTA updates carrying no API host, and what inlined the Google
# client ID as `undefined` (the 12500 sign-in failure). A constant in source
# cannot be forgotten, is reviewable, and has git history.
#
# Rule of thumb for adding something here: if it does not differ between your
# machine and a release, it is not an environment variable — put it in
# `config/constants.js`. If it is a real secret, it belongs here and nowhere
# else. Everything else is a per-machine switch.
# =============================================================================

# ── Firebase App Check debug tokens (secret — required for simulator work) ───
#
# A token registered in the Firebase console mints valid App Check tokens for
# this app, which bypasses iOS App Attest and Android Play Integrity outright.
# Never commit one, never let one reach a shipping artifact.
#
# `app.config.ts` surfaces these into `extra` ONLY when neither
# EAS_BUILD_PROFILE nor RELEASE_CHANNEL is set (default-deny), so a build or an
# OTA publish omits them. `src/services/app-check.ts` reads them back and passes
# them to `provider.configure({ debugToken })` — the env path is the only App
# Check debug-token injection that reliably works on the iOS Simulator with this
# prebuild. Details: docs/explanation/app-check.md
#
# Get one by launching the app with App Check debug logging on and registering
# the printed UUID in Firebase console → App Check → the app → Manage debug
# tokens.
FIREBASE_APP_CHECK_DEBUG_TOKEN_IOS=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
FIREBASE_APP_CHECK_DEBUG_TOKEN_ANDROID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# ── Optional: point this machine at a local API server ───────────────────────
#
# Leave unset for normal work — unset means `PROD_API_URL` from
# `config/constants.js`, and a dev build on the production host shows a
# persistent red "DEV · PRODUCTION API" strip so it is never a surprise.
#
# `resolveBaseUrl()` in `app.config.ts` IGNORES this variable entirely whenever
# EAS_BUILD_PROFILE or RELEASE_CHANNEL names a shipping profile (beta,
# production). Not "defaults over it" — does not read it. A localhost host is
# therefore unrepresentable in a release artifact, no matter what this file
# says, what your shell exports, or whether the release ran through the scripts.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3010

# ── Optional: iOS push entitlement environment ───────────────────────────────
#
# Read at `app.config.ts` → `ios.entitlements["aps-environment"]`. Set to
# `development` to get the sandbox APNs entitlement, which is what a debug build
# installed from Xcode / `expo run:ios` needs to receive push. ANY other value,
# including unset, yields `production` — which is what TestFlight, the App Store
# and every EAS build require, so the safe default is to leave this alone.
#
# Only affects a native build (it is baked into the entitlements at prebuild),
# so changing it means `npx expo prebuild --clean` and a rebuild. It has no
# effect on an OTA publish.
# APP_ENV=development
